# Coroutines, not Handler

Move work and receive callbacks with a `CoroutineScope` or `callbackFlow`. Do not add `android.os.Handler`, `HandlerThread`, `post`, or `postDelayed`.

## Listener APIs

A sensor, broadcast, or other register/unregister callback is a `callbackFlow`. Register inside the flow. Unregister in `awaitClose`. Collect it on `viewModelScope`, `lifecycleScope`, or an injected scope (`SupervisorJob()` plus an injected `CoroutineDispatcher`). Do not use `GlobalScope`.

```kotlin
scope.launch {
    callbackFlow {
        val listener = object : SensorEventListener {
            override fun onSensorChanged(event: SensorEvent) {
                event.values.firstOrNull()?.let { trySend(it) }
            }
            override fun onAccuracyChanged(sensor: Sensor?, accuracy: Int) = Unit
        }
        sensorManager.registerListener(listener, sensor, SensorManager.SENSOR_DELAY_NORMAL)
        awaitClose { sensorManager.unregisterListener(listener) }
    }.collect { distance -> onDistance(distance) }
}
```

Delay with `delay`. Switch threads with `withContext`. RPI's `ProximitySensor` is the reference (`callbackFlow` plus a scope).

## Names that are not this

- Compose `BackHandler` is the system back callback.
- A platform method whose signature requires a `Looper` (older `LocationManager.requestLocationUpdates`) may receive `Looper.getMainLooper()` as that argument. Prefer an `Executor` overload when the platform has one. Do not wrap that call in a `Handler`.
